Introduction
Almost everything we build in MetaTrader is clocked by the calendar. A bar closes every minute, every hour, every day, and our indicators, our models, and our Expert Advisors all sample the market on that fixed grid. But the market does not know what a minute is. During a news release a hundred meaningful moves can happen in a single minute, while over a quiet weekend an hour carries no information at all. Sampling on the clock spends the same attention on both, which means it oversamples noise and undersamples the events that actually move price.
There is an old alternative, rediscovered and formalised by the Olsen group in Zurich: let the price itself decide when time passes. In this intrinsic time, the clock ticks only when the price does something worth noticing, namely a directional change of a chosen size. This single idea turns out to be remarkably productive. The original study reports a set of empirical scaling laws that hold for close to three orders of magnitude and across thirteen currency pairs, and the same group later built on them a counter-trending trading model, the Alpha Engine, which they report is profitable even on a random walk.
In this article we build the full chain in pure MQL5 and test those claims on our own data. We implement the directional-change operator, reproduce the scaling laws on 17.8 million EUR/USD ticks, one pair over one six-month window, against a random walk of the same volatility, and port the Alpha Engine into an Expert Advisor, examining both its random-walk profit and its Strategy Tester run. The two source papers are Glattfelder, Dupuis and Olsen,
Patterns in high-frequency FX data: discovery of 12 empirical scaling laws
(Quantitative Finance, 2011)
, and Golub, Glattfelder and Olsen,
The Alpha Engine: Designing an Automated Trading Algorithm
(2017)
.
We will cover:
Intrinsic Time and the Directional-Change Operator
The Scaling Laws, Verified on Real Ticks
The Alpha Engine: Coastline Trading
Building the Expert Advisor
Strategy Tester Results
Conclusion
Intrinsic Time and the Directional-Change Operator
Fix a threshold, call it delta (for example 0.25%). Start in an
up
mode and track the highest price seen so far, the extreme. As long as new highs keep coming, nothing happens except that the extreme moves up. The moment the price falls by delta from that extreme, we register a directional change: the trend has, by our own definition, turned down. We flip to
down
mode, reset the extreme to the current price, and now wait for a rise of delta to turn us back up.
The confirming move of size delta is only part of the story. After a change confirms, the price usually keeps going in the new direction well beyond the delta that triggered the change. That continuation is the overshoot. A directional change plus the overshoot that follows it is one total move, and the whole price path becomes an alternating sequence of directional-change and overshoot sections. This is the dissection the papers call the coastline. Crucially, the clock advances only at these events, so a stormy hour and a sleepy hour are measured in the same currency: events (Fig. 1).
Fig. 1. A jagged price path resolved into directional-change (solid) and overshoot (dashed) sections at a fixed threshold; the intrinsic-time clock ticks only at the events.
The event condition is a single comparison against the extreme. Writing x for the current price and x_ext for the extreme, a downturn while in up mode is:
(x - x_ext) / x_ext <= -delta
and an upturn while in down mode is the mirror image with +delta. The paper presents this as its Algorithm 2, and we implement it as an online operator: one price at a time, no look-ahead, so the same operator can run unchanged offline over a stored tick history or online over a live feed. The class is CDcOS, and its whole decision lives in the Update method. Alongside detecting the change, it also splits each completed move at its extreme, recording the directional-change leg and the overshoot leg, which is what the scaling laws will measure.
