The two branches are symmetric. In each, the price either extends the extreme, in which case only the overshoot bookkeeping advances, or it retraces by delta, in which case a change fires. On a change, the operator calls its private FillEvent to measure the just-completed move, then resets the reference and flips mode. Everything the scaling laws need, the count of changes and the summed sizes of the legs, is accumulated as it goes, so a bank of these operators can sweep many thresholds over one tick stream in a single pass.
That measurement is the subtle part, and it is worth seeing. At the instant a change confirms, two different moves are being described at once. The extreme still holds the high or low the price ran to since the last change, and the price on record for that last change is still available, so the move that just ended can be split cleanly at its extreme. The overshoot leg runs from the last change to the extreme, and the directional-change leg runs from the extreme to the confirming price. That second leg is at least delta long, and a little longer whenever a single tick jumps past the level. Both are recorded as signed returns, together with how many price-changing ticks and how many whole seconds each leg consumed:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Split the move that just ended at its extreme and record it.     |
//|                                                                  |
//|  Called at the confirming price, before the reset, while x_ext   |
//|  still holds the extreme and x_dc the previous change. The       |
//|  directional-change leg runs x_ext -> confirming price and is    |
//|  the delta-sized move that just fired; the overshoot leg runs    |
//|  x_dc -> x_ext and belongs to the move now ending. Leg lengths   |
//|  come straight from the stamped indices and times.               |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
CDcOS::FillEvent(
const
ENUM_DC_EVENT type,
const
double
price,
const
datetime
time)
  {
   m_event.type     =type;
   m_event.price    =price;
   m_event.time     =time;
   m_event.dc_return=(price-m_x_ext)/m_x_ext;
   m_event.os_return=(m_x_ext-m_x_dc)/m_x_dc;
   m_event.ticks_dc =m_idx-m_idx_ext;
   m_event.ticks_os =m_idx_ext-m_idx_dc;
   m_event.secs_dc  =(
double
)(time-m_t_ext);
   m_event.secs_os  =(
double
)(m_t_ext-m_t_dc);
  }
Recording the overshoot only when the next change fires is what makes the coastline measurable at all: the size of an overshoot is unknowable until its extreme is final, and that happens only once the price has turned by delta. The two stamped indices make each leg's tick and time length a subtraction rather than a second pass over the data. Three details matter later: the running sums behind the scaling laws use absolute returns; a quote that repeats the last price is skipped, so tick counts are counts of price changes; and time stamps are whole seconds, so events inside one second register zero elapsed time.
Because the operator is pure computation with no dependence on bars or history, we can validate it exactly. A short hand-built price path with a large delta produces a known sequence of events whose returns, tick counts, and elapsed seconds can be computed by hand and asserted bit-for-bit; a strictly rising ramp must produce no events at all. Both tests pass, which lets us trust the numbers the next section reports.
The Scaling Laws, Verified on Real Ticks
Once a price path is a sequence of events, we can ask how the statistics of those events change as we vary the threshold delta. The scaling-law paper reports a set of empirical scaling laws that answer exactly this. Three of them tell the whole story and can be measured from the operator alone, with no time interpolation.
The full catalogue is larger. Beyond the count of changes, the paper relates the average and cumulative size of each leg to the threshold, the average number of ticks a move contains, and the average time a move or a change takes, giving eighteen relations of which twelve are independent. The waiting-time laws need a physical clock, and therefore require interpolating price onto a fixed time grid. The laws we focus on here need only the shape of the price path. That is why the operator can measure them directly, and why they are the cleanest to reproduce on a broker's raw tick stream.
The first is the directional-change count law: the number of changes at threshold delta follows a power law,
N(delta) = (delta / C)^E
with an exponent
E
close to -2. In plain terms, halving the threshold produces roughly four times as many events. The second concerns the overshoot. The paper's single most useful empirical fact is that, on average, the overshoot is the same size as the threshold that defined it. The paper states it plainly:
One notable observation was that, on average, a directional change delta is followed by an overshoot omega of the same magnitude, [...] this justifies the procedure of dissecting the price curve into directional-change and overshoot segments of the same size.
Since a total move is a directional change plus its overshoot, this means the average total move is about twice the threshold. The third law is the
coastline
: summing the sizes of every move gives a total path length that grows as a power law of delta with an exponent near -0.94, and it is far longer than intuition suggests, which is the raw material a coastline trader harvests (Fig. 2).
Fig. 2. The two workhorse laws at a glance: the event count scales as roughly the inverse square of the threshold, while the average overshoot roughly matches the threshold that defined it.
To measure a law we sweep a bank of operators over a grid of thresholds, accumulate each threshold's aggregates, and fit a straight line to the log of the response against the log of delta. That is exactly the estimation the paper does with a linear model: from the fitted slope
B
and intercept
A
, the scaling-law form follows with the exponent E = B and the scale C = exp(-A/B). The heart of our fitter, an excerpt from FitPowerLaw, is a plain ordinary-least-squares that also returns an honest error bar on the exponent:
