Driving the fit is a bank of operators, one per threshold, sharing a single pass over the ticks, so a forty-threshold sweep over months of data costs one read. The operators keep only running sums, never the ticks themselves, so memory stays flat no matter how long the history.
A scaling law is a claim, and a claim needs a control. The paper benchmarks every law against a Gaussian random walk, because a pattern that a structureless walk reproduces is a property of the method, not of the market. We do the same, using ALGLIB's high-quality generator, with one change: we size the walk to our own ticks rather than borrow the paper's step, for a reason the results will make clear.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Stream a Gaussian random walk through a bank, as the negative    |
//| control the paper uses.                                          |
//|                                                                  |
//|  The paper benchmarks every law against a GRW, x_{i+1} = x_i +   |
//|  N(0, sigma^2): a pattern the walk reproduces belongs to the     |
//|  method, not to the market. Increments are additive, one second  |
//|  apart and seeded so the run repeats. Size sigma to the market's |
//|  own per-tick volatility, or the step alone distorts the slopes. |
//|  ALGLIB's high-quality generator supplies the normal draws. The  |
