We ran the sweep over 17.8 million EUR/USD real ticks from our broker's history, 2 February to 31 July 2026, pulled in fortnight-long windows with CopyTicksRange. The grid is forty log-spaced thresholds from 0.02% to 2%; a threshold joins a fit once it has produced at least thirty directional changes, which leaves the thirty-two finest (0.02% to about 0.78%), and the control is fitted on that same set. The control takes one step for each of the 17.7 million price changes in the window, with the step volatility measured from those ticks, about 1.09e-5 of the price. To reproduce the results, attach the DcOS_ScalingLaws script to a EUR/USD chart and wait until the tick history is fully synced.
Then open the fit summary it writes to the terminal's Common\Files folder. The script also fits the four secondary tick- and time-based laws, which come out near the paper's values, around +2, and prints them for completeness. The exponents are the scale-invariant fingerprint we compare against the paper's EUR/USD values. Every market fit is a near-straight line, with an R-squared of at least 0.998 and a small standard error (-1.944 +/- 0.007 for the count law), but that only says the relationship is smooth over the fitted range: steadily rising or falling aggregates look straight on log-log axes almost regardless. That is why the control matters.
