Every market exponent sits close to the paper's, with only the overshoot-move slope a little low. Two direct checks describe the dissection itself. The average overshoot is close to the threshold at fine resolutions, 1.025 times delta at a threshold of about 0.05% and 1.049 times at 0.02%, but it drifts down to about 0.74 times at 0.78%, where only a few dozen events remain. So "a total move is about twice delta" is a good approximation at fine thresholds rather than an exact identity. The average daily coastline at about 0.05% measured 3.82%, inside the paper's cross-pair range.
The control is where the result becomes humbling. The volatility-matched walk reproduces the market's exponents: count law -1.974 against -1.944, coastline -0.969 against -0.978. Other seeds move the walk's exponents by a few hundredths (count -1.94 to -2.00, coastline -0.96 to -1.00), more than any single fit's error bar, so gaps of that size are noise. A walk built with the paper's fixed step, about ten times coarser than a EUR/USD price change, gives -1.768 and -0.828, which looks like market structure; it is only the coarse step flattening the slopes, and matching the step closes the gap.
So at the thresholds and over the horizon we can measure on one broker's feed, EUR/USD scales like a random walk of the same volatility. The laws are real and reproducible, but they do not separate the market from noise, and our data cannot support the claim that they reveal hidden structure. The only departure we see, a slight negative correlation between consecutive price changes of about -0.06, is far too weak to move the exponents.
The coastline number is still striking. At a fine 0.05% resolution the price path travels several percent per day, far more than the day's net move. A trend follower sees only the net displacement between two points; a coastline trader sees the whole jagged length in between and aims to bank pieces of it. The matched control shows that this length is not special to the market, which moves the question to where it belongs: whether a particular way of trading the coastline makes money after costs, with its open positions counted. That is the question the rest of the article takes up.
The Alpha Engine: Coastline Trading
If a directional change is, on average, followed by an overshoot of the same size, then after a move of delta the price tends to travel another delta before it turns. A contrarian can exploit this: open against the move at an intrinsic event, and if the price runs further against the position, add to it at the next event, bringing the average entry closer to the market. This is cascading. When the price reverts, close the added pieces one by one at a profit, which is de-cascading (Fig. 3). Because the pieces are only ever added in fixed increments, this is not a martingale; it is a disciplined way to harvest the coastline. The paper's own conclusion is blunt about the surprising consequence:
Despite conventional wisdom, it is in fact possible to 'beat' a random walk. The Alpha Engine produces profitable results even on time series generated by a random walk [...] the procedure of translating a tick-by-tick time series into intrinsic time events skews the odds in one's favour.
The edge is combinatorial. The coastline operator sets its overshoot threshold to delta itself, so after a cascade one more step in the same direction has probability 1/e, which makes the next intrinsic event a de-cascade (price reverts) with probability 1 - 1/e, about 0.63, against roughly 0.37 for a further cascade. Over many events, trimming more often than adding turns the long coastline into a steady stream of realised profit. The word realised matters: the adds that are never trimmed stay open, and we will see shortly that they carry the other side of the ledger.
Two further ingredients make this robust rather than reckless. The first is asymmetric thresholds. In a sustained trend a symmetric operator keeps accumulating inventory on the losing side, so the engine skews its thresholds against the trend as inventory builds: it becomes reluctant to add and eager to trim. The paper motivates this through the notion of market-making indifference prices, and implements it pragmatically as three runners of increasing skew, chosen by the current inventory. While the position is small the symmetric runner drives it; past 15 units of inventory a runner with the adverse threshold widened to twice the favourable one takes over; past 30 units the ratio becomes four. The second ingredient is fractional sizing: when the thresholds are skewed, the unit that is added is subdivided, to a half and then a quarter, so the position grows in smaller, slower steps during exactly the conditions that are dangerous. Together they let the model lean into an ordinary overshoot while refusing to let a genuine trend run the inventory away.
Fig. 3. A long agent cascades (adds) as price falls through successive intrinsic events, then de-cascades (trims at a profit) as price recovers; position size only ever increases by fixed increments.
The trading model needs a slightly richer operator than the scaling-law one. It works in log-price so a level is reached exactly when the log-return crosses the threshold, it keeps separate up and down thresholds so it can be skewed against a trend, and it emits not only the directional change (event code plus or minus 1) but also an overshoot event (plus or minus 2) each time the trend extends by a further step. These overshoot events are the extra intrinsic-time ticks the trader cascades on. The runner is CAeRunner, and its logic is a faithful translation of the reference Java, reading the bid and ask on the executable side of each move.
