Because the thresholds live in log-price, the runner never waits passively: each time the extreme moves it recomputes the exact price at which the next event would fire, so the trader always has a concrete level to rest an order at. The next directional change sits delta from the extreme, and the next overshoot tick sits one overshoot-threshold beyond the current reference; both are simply the extreme or the reference carried along by that log-distance:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Price at which the next directional change would confirm.        |
//|  In down mode we watch for an up move of deltaUp above the       |
//|  extreme; in up mode a down move of deltaDown below it.          |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
CAeRunner::FindExpectedDc(
void
)
  {
if
(m_mode==-
1
)
      m_expected_dc=
MathExp
(
MathLog
(m_extreme)+m_delta_up);
else
m_expected_dc=
MathExp
(
MathLog
(m_extreme)-m_delta_down);
  }
