The two constants are the self-information of each event type: a directional change is common, so its surprise is small (0.0834), while an overshoot step is rarer, so it carries more (2.5257). The standardising constants H1 and H2 are computed once, from the transition probability alone. Note that the liquidity indicator runs on its own, wider overshoot threshold: where the cascade logic registers an overshoot every delta, this indicator registers one only every 2.525729 times delta. With that threshold, the probability that the trend takes one more overshoot step rather than reversing is exp(-2.525729), about 0.08, and from that single number the entropy and its second moment follow:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Theoretical mean H1 and variance H2 of the per-event surprise.   |
//|  H1 is the Shannon entropy of the two-state transition; H2 is    |
//|  its second moment less H1^2 - see the fidelity note above for   |
//|  the sign the reference uses on the second term.                 |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
CAeLocalLiquidity::ComputeH1H2(
void
)
  {
