Profit on a random walk
. The paper's boldest claim can be tested with no market data at all. We stream seeded Gaussian random walks through the full eight-agent engine and read the profit two ways, kept strictly apart from here on:
realised
profit counts closed round trips only, and
total
profit adds the mark-to-market of whatever cascade is still open at the final tick. Across two hundred walks of a million ticks each, the realised profit was positive every single time, averaging +4.33 in the engine's abstract return units: the 1 - 1/e trimming asymmetry books more de-cascades than cascades even in pure noise, exactly as the paper describes.
The total profit tells a different story. The open cascade averaged -4.12, and the total +0.22 with a standard error of 0.36, indistinguishable from zero. The typical walk still finishes ahead, 123 of the 200 with a median of +1.58, but the losers lose large, the worst near -17. This is what theory predicts: on a random walk with no drift, no rule that sees only past prices can have a positive expected total profit. The dissection reshapes the payoff into many small realised gains and occasional large open losses, so the paper's claim holds for realised profit.
