This offline trader is the fidelity anchor for the whole port: a literal translation of the reference, checked by a known-answer self-test, and the random-walk figures above come from the attached Ae_RandomWalk script driving it. The live Expert Advisor in the next section keeps its runners and liquidity indicator verbatim and adapts the execution in two places: how a de-cascade is carried out once real hedged positions replace the imbalanced list, and, as we flag there, the reference's whole-position exit, which the live agent does not reproduce.
Building the Expert Advisor
The reference trades in abstract units against a price feed; the live agent must move real lots. Following the paper's insistence on limit orders, it runs the entire lifecycle in limit orders on a hedging account, where each cascade step is naturally its own position.
The mapping is clean. A long agent keeps a single buy-limit resting at the next downward intrinsic-event level, and each fill opens a position with a take-profit exactly delta above its entry. That take-profit is the de-cascade: as the price falls, the ladder of buy-limits fills and the position cascades; as it recovers, the take-profits trim it one by one. A short agent is the mirror image.
Limit orders remove slippage, not the spread. A buy-limit fills when the ask reaches its level and its take-profit when the bid reaches the target, so the booked profit is level to level but the price must travel delta plus the spread for each round trip. The per-tick logic of one agent, CAeLiveTrader, is:
