One design decision deserves a word on fidelity. In the reference, a de-cascade is a limit order that reduces the net position, a netting-account idea. On a hedging account each cascade position instead carries its own take-profit at delta, and the two are closer than they look: the FindCompensatedOrdersList test above, run with the original delta, closes an order once the trim level is at least delta beyond the order's own level, which is exactly a take-profit at delta. The difference is timing. The reference trims only at its next intrinsic-event level, at or beyond that target, while the live agent closes each position the moment its target is touched, slightly earlier and slightly smaller. The decision core, the runners and the liquidity indicator, is the verbatim port.
There is a second, more consequential difference. The reference also has a whole-position exit: once a position's total return, realised plus open, reaches delta, it closes everything at market. The live agent does not reproduce it. MaxUnits still caps the inventory, but a cascade that runs adverse and never recovers is only halted, not closed, with its take-profits left resting. This is the single largest risk in the live design, and the backtest in the next section shows what it costs.
A practical caveat comes with mapping abstract units to lots. The paper's fractional sizing produces adds as small as a quarter of a unit scaled by the liquidity coefficient, and on a broker with a 0.01-lot step those only survive as distinct sizes when LotPerUnit is large enough, around 0.4, that the smallest add still clears the minimum lot. Below that, the smaller adds are raised to the minimum lot and the fine sizing collapses onto fewer sizes, although the more important threshold skew, which lives in the runners rather than in the volume, is unaffected.
